---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { site, skills } from '../site.config';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="About Matt Chamblin: twelve years of backend and platform engineering at Warner Bros. Discovery, and earlier front-end work at NPR and NBA.com."
>
  <PageHeader label="About" title="A bit <em>about me</em>." />
  <div class="container-page grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_18rem]">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I’m a backend and platform engineer. Most of my work is the content API, the event bus, the CDN rules and the
        release pipeline.
      </p>
      <p>
        I came to engineering sideways. I studied English at Guilford College, then spent ten years teaching technology
        and Spanish.
      </p>
      <p>
        My first full-time engineering job was at NPR. I spent two years on an Agile team building the front end of its
        content distribution portal, and it’s still some of the work I’m proudest of.
      </p>
      <p>
        I then spent twelve years at Turner, WarnerMedia, and Warner Bros. Discovery, the same company under three
        names. I started on the front end of NBA.com and the NBA League Pass storefront, moved to the site and API for
        Turner Classic Movies, and then to the shared publishing platform behind HBO.com, TCM.com, DC.com, and other
        brand sites.
      </p>
      <p>
        On that platform I moved from building features to designing how the pieces fit together. I designed its Kafka
        event bus, led the backend side of moving TCM.com onto it, and designed the publishing path that replaces a
        whole-site rebuild with a single JSON manifest. Along the way I wrote ten architecture decision records and
        reviewed more than 1,700 pull requests.
      </p>
      <p>
        I write my own runbooks and migration plans. I’ve written post-incident reports, and I follow the fixes through.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lately I’ve been working with AI coding tools every day. I built an agent pipeline that triages vulnerability
        tickets and drafts fixes, and leaves the merge decision to a person.
      </p>
      <p>I’m looking for my next backend or platform role.</p>
      {/* TODO: add a line about life outside work so there is a person on the page. */}
      <p>
        The fastest way to reach me is <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="space-y-8">
      {
        skills.map((group) => (
          <div class="border-t border-line pt-4">
            <h2 class="label">{group.group}</h2>
            <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5">
              {group.items.map((item) => (
                <li>{item}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </aside>
  </div>
</Base>
